PRD: @melon-db/db-query – fluent query builder
1. Scope
@melon-db/db-query provides the first-party, type-safe, fluent query API that compiles to the QueryAst defined in @melon-db/db. It has no knowledge of SQLite or React.

2. Goals
Offer a modern, composable query DSL that’s easy to use in services, hooks, and tests.

Become the canonical in-repo query API, with Mango/Prisma as alternate syntaxes compiled to the same AST.

Preserve the spirit of WatermelonDB’s query descriptions (filter, sort, etc.), but in a TypeScript-first builder.

3. Non-goals
No direct DB IO; it should only return QueryAst.

No concern for sync or backend querying (local-only semantics).

Not required to preserve every WatermelonDB query helper 1:1; focus on common patterns.

4. Functional requirements
Provide a QueryBuilder<RecordShape> with:

where(field, op, value)

and(group => ...)

or(group => ...)

orderBy(field, direction?)

limit(n)

skip(n)

include(relation, config?)

toAst(mode?: 'many' | 'one' | 'count'): QueryAst

Provide a QueryFactory:

from<RecordShape>(collection: string): QueryBuilder<RecordShape>

TS generics should:

Restrict field params to keys of RecordShape.

Restrict relation names where possible.

Provide utilities:

byId, byForeignKey, openTasks() style reusable builders.

5. Non-functional requirements
Type safety: Good inference for fields and relations.

Composability: Builders are cheap, immutable, and can be shared.

Testability: Query building can be unit-tested against AST output.

6. Milestones
M0 – API design (1 week)

Write an RFC with sample usage:

simple filter, sort, limit

nested AND/OR

includes

Map common WatermelonDB query usage to this API so migration is straightforward.

M1 – Implementation (2–3 weeks)

Implement builder data structure and methods.

Implement toAst (no adapter/SQL).

Unit tests:

Basic predicates → AST

Nested boolean groups

Order/limit/skip

M2 – Integration with @melon-db/db (1 week)

Wire builder into MelonCollection.query convenience overloads:

collection.query(builder => builder.where(...))

Add examples in docs and playground.

PRD: @melon-db/db-react – React bindings
1. Scope
@melon-db/db-react provides React bindings for MelonDatabase and queries:

Context provider to inject a MelonDatabase.

Hooks to read from collections and run queries.

Hooks to run writes in a safe, Watermelon-like “writer” context.

It plays a similar role to WatermelonDB’s “database passed into React components” patterns and Prisma’s React Native reactive hooks.

2. Goals
Make it trivial to plug @melon-db/db into React Native and React.

Provide a hooks-first API:

useDatabase

useCollection

useQuery

useWriter

Combine reactive subscriptions from the adapter with React renders.

3. Non-goals
No schema or adapter logic (those live in @melon-db/db / @melon-db/db-sqlite).

No devtools UI (bridge only, or keep devtools in its own package).

No direct sync control (sync hooks belong in @melon-db/sync).

4. Functional requirements
<MelonDbProvider db={MelonDatabase}>:

Uses React context.

Optionally takes DevtoolsBridge for debug.

Hooks:

useDatabase<Schema>() => MelonDatabase<Schema>

useCollection<T>(name) => MelonCollection<T>

useQuery<T>(astOrPrepared, options?) => T[]

useQueryCount(astOrPrepared) => number

useFindMany<T>(collection, builderOrArgs):

Works with fluent builder or Prisma-like args.

useMangoQuery<T>(collection, mangoQuery):

On top of @melon-db/db-query-mango.

useWriter() => (fn) => Promise<T>:

Wraps db.write with stable identity for components.

Reactivity:

Uses MelonQueryHandle.observe under the hood.

Unsubscribes on unmount.

Error handling:

Hooks should expose errors via React state or throw for error boundaries.

WatermelonDB patterns for passing Database to components and subscribing to changes should be possible to replicate cleanly here.

5. Non-functional requirements
Performance: Minimal re-renders; consider selectors or select option for useQuery.

Ergonomics: API should feel natural to modern RN devs used to TanStack Query / Prisma hooks.

Compatibility: Works in RN and web React.

6. Milestones
M0 – Hook surface RFC (1 week)

Define final signatures, including options object for useQuery (enabled/select).

Decide whether useFindMany wraps fluent builder or Prisma args, or both.

M1 – Provider + basic hooks (2–3 weeks)

Implement MelonDbProvider, useDatabase, useCollection, useWriter.

Implement useQuery for PreparedQuery only (no builders yet).

Add example: simple list screen in apps/playground-rn.

M2 – Higher-level hooks + integration (2–3 weeks)

Implement useFindMany, useMangoQuery.

Add memoization/select logic.

Example: filtered list, searching, etc.

Suggested order from here
Given you’re starting with @melon-db/db + @melon-db/db-sqlite:

Implement @melon-db/db M0/M1 (with an in-memory adapter).

Implement @melon-db/db-sqlite SQL generator + Node adapter.

Implement @melon-db/db-query (so you can author nice queries).

Implement @melon-db/db-react (so you can dogfood this in RN).

Then tackle Mango (@melon-db/db-query-mango) and/or Prisma (@melon-db/db-prisma).

